 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n### [Bug]: Descriptor Binding Issue in FunctionModel\n\n#### Bug Summary\n\nIt seems there's an issue with the descriptor binding in the `FunctionModel` class. When attempting to bind descriptors, the expected behavior is not achieved, leading to unexpected results.\n\n#### Code for Reproduction\n\nTo reproduce the issue, you can use the following snippet:\n\n```python\nfrom astroid.interpreter.objectmodel import FunctionModel\nfrom astroid import nodes\n\n# Create a mock function node\nfunc_node = nodes.FunctionDef(name=\"mock_func\", lineno=1, col_offset=0)\n\n# Create a FunctionModel instance\nfunction_model = FunctionModel(func_node)\n\n# Attempt to infer call result with invalid arguments\ntry:\n    result = list(function_model.infer_call_result(None))\n    print(\"Inference Result:\", result)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n```\n\n#### Actual Outcome\n\nThe code raises an error when attempting to infer the call result with invalid arguments, which is not the expected behavior.\n\n#### Expected Outcome\n\nThe expected outcome is that the function should handle invalid arguments gracefully without raising an error, possibly by returning a default or empty result.\n\n#### Additional Information\n\nThis issue was observed on a Linux environment with Python 3.10.16. The problem seems to be related to the handling of arguments in the `infer_call_result` method of the `FunctionModel` class.\n</issue>\n",
